From: Marc Zyngier <maz@kernel.org>
Date: Thu, 28 May 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] KVM: arm64: Remove stage-2 read permission fault check

The check for "unexpected L2 read permission error" in user_mem_abort()
is overly strict and triggers when L1 creates a write-only mapping, then
upgrades it to read-write without flushing the TLB. This results in a
genuine read fault at stage-2, but the check treats it as fatal, causing
a VM crash.

Remove the check entirely. The fault is handled normally by the rest of
the function and will resolve correctly once the TLB is updated. This
matches upstream commit e9c7a1e1a (KVM: arm64: Remove stage 2 read
fault check).

Signed-off-by: Marc Zyngier <maz@kernel.org>
---
virt/kvm/arm/kvm/mmu.c | 6 ------
1 file changed, 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/virt/kvm/arm/kvm/mmu.c b/virt/kvm/arm/kvm/mmu.c
index 000000000000..111111111111 100644
--- a/virt/kvm/arm/kvm/mmu.c
+++ b/virt/kvm/arm/kvm/mmu.c
@@ -1510,12 +1510,6 @@ static int user_mem_abort(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu, phys_addr_t fault_ipa,
	exec_fault = kvm_vcpu_trap_is_exec_fault(vcpu);
	VM_BUG_ON(write_fault && exec_fault);

-	/* This check is too strict and breaks nested virt when L1
-	 * upgrades a write-only mapping to read-write without a TLB
-	 * flush. Remove it to avoid crashing L2. */
-	if (fault_is_perm && !write_fault && !exec_fault) {
-		kvm_err("Unexpected L2 read permission error\n");
-		return -EFAULT;
-	}

	if (!is_protected_kvm_enabled())
		memcache = &vcpu->arch.mmu_page_cache;
-- 
2.34.1
